    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scientists generally argue in favor of mandatory vaccination protocols, citing the overwhelming scientific consensus that vaccination is the most effective way to prevent outbreaks and protect public health. They emphasize the potential risks and consequences of vaccine-preventable diseases, such as measles and smallpox, which can be severe and even fatal. On the other hand, civil liberties advocates typically argue that mandatory vaccination infringes upon individual rights to autonomy, bodily autonomy, and informed decision-making, raising concerns about government overreach and coercion. They also often point to the cases of vaccines being proven to cause harm in rare instances, as well as the existence of Medical Exemptions that provide alternatives for those with legitimate health concerns.",
